    {
      "hint_id": 2,
      "train_position": 28,
      "train_qid": "30302",
      "hint": "For \"all roots lie on one circle\" problems, take moduli before hunting roots. From (z-a)^n = c(z-b)^n get |z-a| = k|z-b| with k = |c|^(1/n). TRAP: |c|*|z-b|^n means c is NOT raised to the n-th power; compute k as a plain number and check k^n = |c| before continuing, since a wrong k gives a wrong but perfectly self-consistent circle. Then set z = x+iy, square, collect the x^2+y^2 terms, divide by their coefficient, complete the square, read off center and radius (an Apollonius circle; k=1 degenerates to a line). Verify against the ORIGINAL equation, never your derived one: solve (z-a)/(z-b) = k for its real root and check its distance to your center equals your radius. Then box.",
