    {
      "hint_id": 5,
      "train_position": 70,
      "train_qid": "30418",
      "hint": "When unknown points on a fixed curve y=g(x) are tied by a known displacement (u,v), reduce to ONE unknown: let the free vertex be (t, g(t)) and its partner (t+u, g(t)+v), then solve the single scalar equation g(t+u) = g(t) + v.\n\nFor a logarithmic curve, convert that additive offset to a multiplicative one BEFORE cross-multiplying: log_c(B) - log_c(A) = v means B/A = c^v. Flipping the direction or sign of that power is the dominant failure mode.\n\nIf you hit an impossible identity or a negative discriminant, you flipped the offset: recheck only that conversion line. Do not conclude the configuration is impossible, and do not restart from scratch.\n\nDiscard roots that reproduce a given vertex (degenerate). Finish with the shoelace determinant in cyclic order.",
