      "hint": "When the constraint is a commuting identity like p(q(x)) = q(p(x)) with unknown coefficients, do not expand the composition: its degree is the product of the degrees, and drowning in that algebra is the main way these problems are lost. Instead posit an affine link q = a*p + b, testing the simplest cases a = ±1 first, and fix b from the given point values. The identity then reduces to \"p commutes with an affine involution\", which forces p to be antisymmetric about that map's fixed point m: p(m+t) + p(m-t) is constant. Use that symmetry to produce the requested outputs directly instead of solving for every coefficient. Expect one leftover free coefficient; verify the requested values do not depend on it, then set it to something convenient and check the identity numerically at two points.",
